e is True` bypass, `_setup_logger` reading `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`).\n   - Edit `deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py` (`logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`).\n   - Run final full `python -m pytest -q` and ensure it passes.\n   - Generate `run.log`, `patch.diff` (three files), maintain `modified-source/`.\n   - Update plan statuses and call `final_answer` after verification.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   Immediately before the summary request, the assistant was trying to confirm the course tests' expectations for the new formatter argument by searching the repository for `max_message_chars` (two failed/blank attempts: `print(search_code(\"max_message_chars\", [\"tests\", \"deepinfer\"], 40))` and `hits = search_code(\"max_message_chars\", [\".\"], 40); print(hits)`), after having completed the baseline pytest (`rc=1`, \"50 failed, 10 passed in 2.37s\") and having all required mechanisms exposed (environ 3/3, logger 3/3, stats_logger 4/4) with full-read hashes registered. Course progress states `phase=edit`, `baseline_done=1`, `remaining source changes: deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py, deepinfer/environ.py, deepinfer/logger.py`, `requests_left: 14`. Plan titles/order to preserve: \"Read required source files\" (doing), \"Run baseline pytest\" (todo → should be marked done), \"Edit environ.py, logger.py, stats_logger.py\" (todo), \"Run final pytest and produce artifacts\" (todo).\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Proceed directly to the first edits (no more exploratory test searching needed), applying scoped `replace_text` calls with the registered hashes: add `int_or_none_min` factory plus `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(32)` in environ.py, add `max_message_chars` + truncation logic and `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` in `_setup_logger` in logger.py, and change `logger.info(m)` to `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})` in stats_logger.py, then run `python -m pytest -q`. This is directly in line with the progress directive: \"Course progress before this request ... {\"phase\":\"edit\",\"next_requirement\":\"remaining source changes: deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py, deepinfer/environ.py, deepinfer/logger.py\",\"remaining_count\":3 ... first_write_reading_required\":true ...}\" and the contract \"When progress says phase=edit, the initial reading and baseline are satisfied. Apply a scoped edit and verify.\"\n</summary>", "reasoning_content": "Let me look at the current state. I have read the mechanisms. Now I need to make the edits. I have evidence for the relevant parts. Let me proceed to edit.\n\nFirst environ.py: add `_int_or_none_min` helper and the config item.\n\nLet me check `EnvVar.int_or_none` exists — yes. But I need min 32 validation and error message containing variable name (which is added by `__get__` wrapper: `f\"{self.name}: {e}\"`). So raising ValueError with message like \"must be >= 32\" gets prefixed with the name. Good.\n\nEmpty string: `int(\"\")` raises ValueError -> message \"invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''\" — wrapped with name. Good.\n\nNow write the descriptor:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(min_value: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < min_value:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value}, must be >= {min_value}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nAnd config: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(32)` in Logging section.\n\nNote: `from __future__ import annotations` is present, so `EnvVar[int | None]` annotation in factory is fine.\n\nNow logger.py formatter. Requirements:\n\n- `__init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None)`.\n- validate: None ok; bool not allowed; must be int >= 32.\n- format: apply args substitution first, then length count. Need access to record.getMessage() to compute message. But must not modify record.msg/args. `super().format(record)` sets `record.message = record.getMessage()` — actually `logging.Formatter.format` sets `record.message = record.getMessage()`. That's the standard behavior and allowed (it's what the original does too). The requirement says don't rewrite msg/args/exc_info/stack_info. Setting record.message is normal.\n\nSo plan: in format, first compute the truncated message and temporarily... hmm. The tricky part: `super().format(record)` formats using record.message and appends exc_info/stack_info. We want to truncate message after arg substitution but keep prefix and traceback intact.\n\nApproach:\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        try:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n        except Exception:\n            message = None\n        if message is not None and len(message) > max_chars:\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, max_chars)\n            saved = (record.msg, record.args)\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = ()\n            try:\n                s = self._format_with_prefix(record)\n            finally:\n                record.msg, record.args = saved\n            return s\n    return self._format_with_prefix(record)\n```\nWait — but modifying record.msg temporarily and restoring could be racy in threads, but the requirement says \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\" — temporarily swapping during format and restoring... risky but functionally fine for a single-thread handler? Actually a shared LogRecord given to multiple handlers: handlers are called sequentially in `Logger.callHandlers`, so no concurrency for the same record within one thread. But cleaner: avoid mutating record at all by overriding record's message.\n\nBetter approach: `s = super().format(record)` gives full string with prefix and traceback. Instead, compute pieces ourselves? Requirement: \"沿用原有多行前缀、时间精度、颜色及异常处理\" — reusing existing logic is best.\n\nAlternative: don't pass record to super; instead compute:\n\nActually the simplest, non-mutating approach: build the formatted string via super().format(record) but make the message short. To do so without mutating the record, we can use `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` copy? That copies and `record2.msg = truncated`, `record2.args = ()`. But exc_info contains traceback objects — fine. `formatter.format` caches exc_text on the record: `if record.exc_info ... record.exc_text`. With a copy, exc_text caching doesn't propagate to the original record — minor.\n\nHmm, but a copy via `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)`: makeLogRecord creates a LogRecord with `LogRecord(None, None, \"\", 0, \"\", (), None, None)` then `__dict__.update(dict)`. That works and is a documented approach.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the truncation must happen after `%s` substitution — using record.getMessage() on the copy gives the same. Also multi-line prefix logic in our format uses `record.message`. Since `super().format()` sets `record.message` on the copy (the record we pass), and we use `record.message` in the newline logic — we should use the copy consistently.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```python\n_TRUNCATION_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    marker = _TRUNCATION_MARKER\n    remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return marker[:max_chars]\n    head_len = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail_len = remaining - head_len\n    tail = message[-tail_len:] if tail_len > 0 else \"\"\n    return message[:head_len] + marker + tail\n```\n\nCheck budget 32: remaining = 32-15 = 17; head = 9, tail = 8. ✓ (marker \"...<omitted>...\" — count: \"...\" 3 + \"<omitted>\" 9 + \"...\" 3 = 15 ✓).\n\nAlso multi-line: truncation happens before newline prefixing, so newlines in truncated message get prefixed properly. Fine.\n\nNow formatter:\n\n```python\nclass NewLineFormatter(logging.Formatter):\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nHmm, tests: \"test_invalid_budget[64] - TypeError: New...\" from the baseline run — that's an existing failing test expecting TypeError? Let me check the test to be sure about the expected exception type. Let me look at tests directory. The requirement says raise ValueError. But baseline test names showed \"est_invalid_budget[64] - TypeError: New\" meaning that param currently fails with TypeError because the kwarg isn't accepted. So tests expect ValueError eventually. Let me check tests to confirm which values are invalid (e.g. True, 31, \"64\"?). The spec says: 其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError. So non-int (e.g. float 64.0?) — spec says \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数\" — accept int >= 32. I'll raise ValueError for anything else, including float. Maybe tests pass 64.0 expecting ValueError or accepting? Safer to follow spec strictly: only int (non-bool) >= 32 accepted.\n\nNow format implementation:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n                record = _copy_record_with_message(record, _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars))\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\n\n`record.getMessage()` could raise if args mismatched — original code also raises in super().format, so propagating is fine.\n\n`_copy_record_with_message`:\n```python\ndef _copy_record_with_message(record, message):\n    clone = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n    clone.msg = message\n    clone.args = ()\n    return clone\n```\nHmm but `record.__dict__` — makeLogRecord does `rv.__dict__.update(dict)`, which shares objects in dict (values) but dict itself is new. Then setting clone.msg doesn't affect record.msg. Good.\n\nWait, but exc_text caching: if the original record was already formatted by another handler (e.g. truncated one first then full)... The requirement says \"同一记录随后交给完整处理器，仍须保留完整消息\". Since we don't mutate the original, fine. Note also `record.message` on original gets set by full formatter later — fine.\n\nOne more subtlety: the clone's `exc_text` might be already set from a previous formatting — fine.\n\nAlso `record.message` attribute: after `super().format(clone)`, clone.message is the truncated message. Our newline logic uses `record.message` — but inside format we reassigned `record` to clone, so that's consistent. But careful: hyper-precision formatTime uses record.msecs — clone has it.\n\nNow the default init:\n\n```python\ndef _setup_logger() -> None:\n    global _default_handler\n    ...\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n        max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n    )\n```\n\nBut importing environ into logger: check for circular imports. environ.py imports only stdlib. logger.py importing `from deepinfer.environ import envs` — does environ import logger? No. Safe. Use `from deepinfer.environ import envs` at top of logger.py? Or local import inside `_setup_logger` to avoid cycles. Top-level is fine; but let me be careful about `deepinfer/__init__.py` importing logger... environ imports nothing from package, so no cycle.\n\nThen stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nLet me now check tests to confirm details before editing. Testing isn't required but useful — quick search on tests for \"max_message_chars\".\n\nLet me do a search and then edit. Actually, to save requests, let's search tests and read logger relevant lines in one action... I've already read logger 1-95, 110-174 (mechanisms exposed). Let me just search tests for expectations, then edit all three files in one action? Edits must be verified; I can do multiple replace_text calls in one action.\n\nLet me first search the tests."}, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop"}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 10135, "completion_tokens": 8806, "total_tokens": 18941, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 4096}, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 2459}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens": 4096, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens": 6039}, "system_fingerprint": "bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}, "classification": "ok"}
